      {
        "category": "summary",
        "text": "Security update for python-PyJWT",
        "title": "Title of the patch"
      },
      {
        "category": "description",
        "text": "This update for python-PyJWT fixes the following issues:\n\n- CVE-2026-101917: attacker traffic can amplify outbound requests to the configured JWKS endpoint (bsc#1283060).\n- CVE-2026-101918: unauthenticated request can raise an exception that may produce an HTTP 500 response (bsc#1283061).\n- CVE-2026-102267: forwarded credentials may be disclosed or verification keys may be substituted (bsc#1283064).\n- CVE-2026-102268: an attacker who knows the public key can forge authenticated HMAC tokens (bsc#1283065).\n- CVE-2026-102269: raw-token revocation checks can fail to recognize an equivalent modified token (bsc#1283066).\n- CVE-2026-102270: an attacker can cause intensive CPU consumption (bsc#1283067).\n- CVE-2026-102271: an attacker who knows the public key can forge authenticated HMAC tokens (bsc#1283068).\n- CVE-2026-102274: Malformed RSA JWK aborts parsing of an entire JWK Set (bsc#1283071).\n- CVE-2026-102275: an attacker may use a stolen sender-constrained token without the legitimate private key\n  (bsc#1283072).\n- CVE-2026-103001: reused mutable options mapping can cause verification checks bypass on subsequent decode calls\n  (bsc#1283829).\n",
        "title": "Description of the patch"
      },
